  ✓ CONFIRMED: Spot-price sandwich attack against Limbo secondary-reward li
    Justification: Upon reviewing Limbo.claimSecondaryRewards and 
UniswapHelper.buyFlanAndBurn, I confirmed that the alleged spot-price sandwich 
vulnerability is exploitable. claimSecondaryRewards is publicly callable, 
accepts tokens in calibration or crossedOver state, transfers the entire 
Limbo-held balance to the AMM helper, and invokes buyFlanAndBurn without any 
caller-supplied minimum output, deadline, or price bound. The helper obtains the
pair through the configured factory, reads the pair's current token balances as 
reserves, computes amountOut using the instantaneous constant-product formula, 
transfers the input to the pair, and calls swap. An attacker can front-run this 
transaction with a large input-token sale, moving the pair price against Limbo; 
the Limbo transaction then executes at the manipulated rate, after which the 
attacker can back-run with the reverse trade. The Uniswap pair lock only 
prevents reentrancy within one transaction and does not prevent transaction 
ordering or cross-transaction sandwiching. Although the helper's migration path 
has separate quote-stability checks, those checks do not apply to 
buyFlanAndBurn. Consequently, the implementation provides no effective 
protection against extractable value from Limbo's balance.


